{"text": "An adapted program keeps the provincial learning standards but changes how the student accesses instruction or demonstrates learning. Adaptations can include assistive technology, accessible materials, different pacing, environmental changes, additional time, or an alternate response format. A modified program is different: the learning goals are substantially different from the provincial standards, and progress is assessed against individualized IEP goals.\n\nFor this fictional profile, the decision is: Adapted program. Olivia is working toward Grade 6 learning standards. Access barriers are addressed through assistive technology, flexible pacing, accessible environments, and alternative response formats; the learning standards remain unchanged.\n\nExamples include speech-to-text and digital submissions; extended time and rest breaks; accessible lab equipment; and assessment by oral or recorded response. These examples show why the decision must be based on the learning goals in each subject, not on the designation itself.\n\nTo conclude, an effective student profile is specific, strengths-based, respectful, and immediately usable. It explains how the student communicates, the conditions that support learning, the barriers staff should anticipate, and the actions that increase participation and independence. Most importantly, it preserves high expectations while providing the individualized support the student needs. Thank you. We are ready for questions.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-A-06.mp3", "words": [{"text": "An", "start": 0.1125, "end": 0.2375, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 2}, {"text": "adapted", "start": 0.25, "end": 0.775, "start_char": 3, "end_char": 10}, {"text": "program", "start": 0.7875, "end": 1.325, "start_char": 11, "end_char": 18}, {"text": "keeps", "start": 1.3375, "end": 1.6625, "start_char": 19, "end_char": 24}, {"text": "the", "start": 1.675, "end": 1.775, "start_char": 25, "end_char": 28}, {"text": "pr